 "productionNotes": "Two production totals for the 1970-1996 run circulate and they do not agree. Goodwood Road and Racing and the Classics World buyer's guide both state 317,615 cars; Wikipedia states 326,070 over the same period. The gap of 8,455 is not explained by either camp, and neither figure is sourced to a Solihull build record that could be inspected, so no single total is asserted here. One possible but unverified reading is that the counts differ over the knocked-down assembly carried out in Australia between 1979 and 1983 and in Venezuela between 1973 and 1981, but no source consulted here says so.\n\nThe end date is also stated inconsistently. Wikipedia, Goodwood and Classics World all run the car to 1996, the Classic having been kept in production alongside the P38A that replaced it in 1994; Adrian Flux's history instead ends the first generation in 1994 at the P38A's launch. The 'Classic' suffix itself is retrospective, applied from 1994 to separate the old car from the new one.\n\nVariant counts are firmer than the grand total because the runs are smaller and better documented. The 1981 In Vogue was a limited edition of 1,000 cars in Vogue Blue, at least 250 of them United Kingdom deliveries, followed by a 1982 automatic series and 325 four-doors in Derwent Blue for 1983. Land Rover's own Heritage Collection records 200 CSKs, 49 of them automatic; Classic & Sports Car, working from a survivors' census, agrees on 200 built and identifies 74 known to survive. Collecting Cars puts long-wheelbase LSE production at approximately 3,000. The two-door body was dropped from the United Kingdom price list in 1984 and continued mainly for France, which is why surviving right-hand-drive two-doors of the later 1980s are scarce.",
